{
  "schema": "ora.toolkit-tool.v1",
  "name": "vision_ground",
  "description": "Retrieve up to three inspectable text-grounded target proposals from one selected current RGB-D camera. Use when: task language names an object or part and the scene-level search space should be reduced before the policy makes its own identity decision; begin with the widest useful view, then choose head, front or a useful wrist to resolve a concrete ambiguity. Treat the returned set as recall-oriented proposals: top rank and backend score are within-call ordering signals, not an oracle. Effect: read-only and creates one separate observation-bound target_ref per candidate. Returns: ranked candidates, source camera, bounding boxes, mask-area/depth summaries and numbered overlay; compare all plausible candidates against visible colour, shape, scale, location and surrounding context before selecting one. Does not: choose the correct candidate, guarantee that rank 0 is foreground, combine cameras, expose object ground truth, calibrate scores across calls, or prove absence when current coverage misses the target. On error: Refused: max_candidates outside 1 to 3, before any request is sent; text that is empty after trimming or longer than 120 characters; a camera outside this tool's enum; no grounding service (grounding_unavailable); a request the grounding service rejects (invalid_arguments, with the service's detail). Reject a candidate whose overlay or mask is dominated by table, robot, background or an implausibly large region even when its score is highest; inspect another returned proposal, rephrase a short visible description, switch current camera, or use vision_segment only after you can point safely inside the intended instance. Selection check: compare the requested task referent with every candidate crop and the original view; rank 0 and a high score do not authenticate identity. Separate an instance hypothesis from an observed property. Failed grasping is not evidence that the intended object is static or that a different object is the target. Revise a mistaken association when the visible evidence contradicts it. Boundary and mask/depth diagnostics describe observability, not semantic truth. Localization also carries a single-view support estimate: support_z_m is the height of the surface the target stands on, fitted to depth pixels just outside its mask and evaluated under it; height_above_support_m is the visible top's height above that surface; mid_height_z_m is the midpoint between them; support_point_count counts the pixels that support the fit, and the three heights are null when no support is visible. center_xyz_m is a visible-surface median, so from an oblique or overhead camera it sits near the top; for a parallel-jaw side grasp, use mid_height_z_m as the height reference instead. The estimate is not ground truth: a neighbouring object, a container wall or a hidden base can bias it.",
  "inputSchema": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "camera": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": [
          "overhead",
          "front",
          "wrist_left",
          "wrist_right"
        ],
        "description": "Exact current native-resolution RGB-D source: overhead for central top-down geometry, front for visible frontal appearance, or a wrist camera when its current pose supplies useful detail."
      },
      "text": {
        "type": "string",
        "minLength": 1,
        "maxLength": 120,
        "description": "Short visible object or part description, never a simulator identifier."
      },
      "max_candidates": {
        "type": [
          "integer",
          "null"
        ],
        "minimum": 1,
        "maximum": 3,
        "default": 3,
        "description": "Maximum ranked regions to return, 1 to 3, default 3. Three is what the grounding service answers per call, so a larger number is refused before the request is sent rather than returned as an error you cannot act on. Every returned region gets its own close-up preview; nothing caps previews across a step, and no count of images already in the conversation refuses this call. Fewer regions answer faster: three cost roughly 479 milliseconds against 244 for one. Null means omitted."
      }
    },
    "required": [
      "camera",
      "text"
    ],
    "additionalProperties": false
  },
